  script ordering, which is the existing convention but not a good one.

  [1mB. Convert the page to ES modules.[0m Same [38;5;153mtracking.js[39m, but with [38;5;153mexport function getTrackingId()[39m, and [38;5;153mindex.html[39m switches
  to [38;5;153m<script type="module" src="app.js">[39m with a real [38;5;153mimport[39m.
  [3mWins because[0m it gives genuine module boundaries and explicit dependencies, which pays off as forms multiply. [3mCosts:[0m ES
  modules won't load over [38;5;153mfile://[39m — opening [38;5;153mindex.html[39m by double-clicking stops working and you need a local HTTP
  server. That's a change to how you run the app, not just how it's written. Worth it if you're adding a build step
  anyway; premature if not.

  [1mC. Centralized tracked-submit helper.[0m A helper that all form submissions route through, injecting the id so no
  individual form handler deals with it.
  [3mWins because[0m "other forms will need it later" gets solved once. [3mCosts:[0m this is YAGNI — it's infrastructure for forms
  that don't exist, designed against requirements you haven't stated. I'd rather build A now and extract this shape when
  the second form actually arrives and shows us what it needs.

  [1mDiscarded:[0m having [38;5;153mlogin[39m read the id internally rather than accept it. Fewer moving parts, but your answer was explicit
  that it should be a real parameter, and an internal read would make [38;5;153mlogin[39m untestable without stubbing storage.

  [1mI recommend A.[0m It's the only one that adds no new runtime requirement, and B's module boundaries are a refactor you
  can do later without touching this decision.

  Two wrinkles I'll handle in whichever we pick, flagged now because they're easy to miss: [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m access [1mthrows[0m in
  some privacy modes rather than returning null, and [38;5;153mcrypto.randomUUID()[39m only exists in secure contexts — over [38;5;153mfile://[39m
  or plain [38;5;153mhttp://[39m it's undefined. Both need fallbacks or the login form breaks outright for those users.
